The file /etc/crypttab.initramfs will be added to the initramfs as a regular
crypttab file. Each device listed there will be unlocked at boot. This is
especially important for an encrypted root partition - the system needs to be
aware of its location and how to unlock it. It must be added to
crypttab.initramfs, otherwise it will remain locked and boot will fail.

A crypttab file looks similar to an fstab file but is used for unlocking
volumes, not for mounting them. As with an fstab file, each line represents a
volume and consists of four fields:
    - volume-name: the name of the mapping the unlocked volume will take
    - encrypted device: the path to the encrypted volume or its device UUID if
        starting with "UUID="
    - key-file: the path to the key-file, can be "none" or "-" if unlocked via
        password entry or a method specified in the next field
    - options: options for unlocking the volume, separated by comma%1
